  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts. It typechecks, but I couldn't run it or build it with `vite build`, so I haven't seen a rendered frame or checked the look.\n\n- **Island:** flat-shaded terrain built from non-indexed triangles with a per-face normal and colour. It has a wavy shoreline, a sand/grass/rock palette and jittered vertices. About 34 instanced pine trees (a trunk plus two cones each) and 22 instanced rock boxes sit on it. No model files.\n- **Water:** a 300×300 grid with a custom GLSL material. It has vertex waves, faceted normals from screen-space derivatives, and a fresnel mix with the sky colour. It also has a sun/moon glint, a shallow-to-deep gradient, an animated foam ring that follows the shoreline, and a distance fade to the sky colour.\n- **Day/night:** one cycle takes 20 seconds and starts at hour 9. `sky.preetham` with stars follows the sun. The sun and moon directional lights move and change intensity and colour. The hemisphere light dims at night but stays on, so the scene is never black. Fog colour and the water shader's sun direction, sun colour, sky colour and ambient level are updated every frame.\n- **Camera:** an orbit camera at [0, 12, 36] looks at [0, 3, 0] with `autoRotate` 0.1 rad/s.\n- **Contract:** it draws into `#app` and sets `window.__EVAL_READY__` after `app.ready()` and one animation frame.\n\nThe night colours and the water's fade to the horizon colour are the places most likely to need a tweak once you see them. The sky colour I feed the water and fog is an approximation of the preetham sky, so a faint seam at the horizon is possible.\n\n",
